1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,562
Topic five covered point and line defects.

2
00:00:03,562 --> 00:00:08,749
Now the area and volume defects, and how we actually see them under a microscope.

3
00:00:11,869 --> 00:00:14,222
Most materials are polycrystalline.

4
00:00:14,222 --> 00:00:17,416
During solidification, small crystals nucleate in the

5
00:00:17,416 --> 00:00:20,666
liquid with random orientations, and they grow.

6
00:00:21,086 --> 00:00:25,097
When solidification is complete, the grains impinge on each other.

7
00:00:25,097 --> 00:00:28,326
The junctions where they meet are the grain boundaries.

8
00:00:32,126 --> 00:00:37,419
A grain boundary comes from the crystallographic mismatch where two grains meet.

9
00:00:37,419 --> 00:00:41,312
A large misorientation gives a high angle grain boundary.

10
00:00:41,312 --> 00:00:44,111
A small one gives a low angle boundary.

11
00:00:44,531 --> 00:00:48,967
A low angle boundary can be described as an array of dislocations.

12
00:00:48,967 --> 00:00:51,824
Edge dislocations make a tilt boundary, and

13
00:00:51,824 --> 00:00:54,741
screw dislocations make a twist boundary.

14
00:00:55,161 --> 00:00:57,744
Atoms at a boundary are less tightly bonded and

15
00:00:57,744 --> 00:01:00,931
less densely packed, with a lower coordination.

16
00:01:00,931 --> 00:01:04,393
That costs energy, called the grain boundary energy.

17
00:01:04,813 --> 00:01:07,768
So grain boundaries are more chemically reactive,

18
00:01:07,768 --> 00:01:10,445
and impurities tend to segregate to them.

19
00:01:10,445 --> 00:01:15,407
And a fine grained material has much more boundary area than a coarse grained one.

20
00:01:19,207 --> 00:01:22,133
Coarse grains can be seen with the naked eye.

21
00:01:22,133 --> 00:01:25,790
That is macrostructure, like this high purity lead ingot,

22
00:01:25,790 --> 00:01:29,559
aluminum street light posts, or galvanized garbage cans.

23
00:01:29,979 --> 00:01:33,040
Usually, though, grains are microscopic.

24
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:37,325
The surface is ground and polished to a mirror finish, then etched with a

25
00:01:37,325 --> 00:01:41,387
chemical reagent that attacks the grain boundaries preferentially.

26
00:01:41,807 --> 00:01:44,318
The etched features scatter the incident light

27
00:01:44,318 --> 00:01:47,266
differently, and that creates the contrast.

28
00:01:47,266 --> 00:01:51,633
That answers the slide's question: etching does not magnify anything.

29
00:01:51,633 --> 00:01:53,816
It makes the surface non-uniform.

30
00:01:57,616 --> 00:02:02,826
A stacking fault is a planar defect: a mistake in the stacking sequence.

31
00:02:02,826 --> 00:02:07,472
Recall that F C C stacks A B C A B C, and H C P stacks A B A B.

32
00:02:07,891 --> 00:02:13,036
If one plane is missing, the sequence reads A B C A B, then A again.

33
00:02:13,036 --> 00:02:16,446
The atoms are still on proper sites, but a thin H C

34
00:02:16,446 --> 00:02:19,731
P like layer appears inside the F C C crystal.

35
00:02:20,152 --> 00:02:25,735
Because the mistake is small, its energy is low, so stacking faults are common.

36
00:02:25,735 --> 00:02:28,288
They form during solidification and plastic

37
00:02:28,288 --> 00:02:32,268
deformation, and they affect how easily a metal deforms.

38
00:02:32,268 --> 00:02:37,494
In high resolution T E M, the fault shows as a break in the regular stacking.

39
00:02:41,293 --> 00:02:44,536
Three dimensional defects are called volume defects.

40
00:02:44,536 --> 00:02:49,044
Inclusions, like these rutile needles in sapphire. Precipitates.

41
00:02:49,044 --> 00:02:53,551
Porosity, or voids, like gas pores in a casting. And cracks.

42
00:02:57,451 --> 00:03:01,769
Now the microscopes. An optical microscope uses light, and it is

43
00:03:01,769 --> 00:03:05,051
limited to about two thousand times magnification.

44
00:03:05,472 --> 00:03:09,972
Electron microscopes use electrons, with wavelengths around zero point

45
00:03:09,972 --> 00:03:14,471
zero zero three nanometers instead of about five hundred for light.

46
00:03:14,471 --> 00:03:19,640
So they resolve features down to the atomic scale, around a million times.

47
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:23,747
The scanning electron microscope studies surfaces directly.

48
00:03:23,747 --> 00:03:28,662
It has a large depth of field, so much of a rough sample is in focus at once.

49
00:03:28,662 --> 00:03:31,567
That makes it the tool for fracture surfaces.

50
00:03:31,987 --> 00:03:35,206
The transmission electron microscope sends electrons

51
00:03:35,206 --> 00:03:38,858
through very thin, electron transparent samples.

52
00:03:38,858 --> 00:03:43,314
Contrast comes from diffraction, so grains in different orientations

53
00:03:43,314 --> 00:03:47,895
look different, and high resolution T E M shows atomic columns.

54
00:03:48,315 --> 00:03:52,410
The scanning probe microscope traces a tiny probe across the surface

55
00:03:52,410 --> 00:03:56,626
to map its topography, from the atomic level up to microns.

56
00:03:57,046 --> 00:04:01,839
And the slide's question: why did T E M and S E M appear in the nineteen

57
00:04:01,839 --> 00:04:06,442
thirties and sixties, but S P M only in the nineteen eighties?

58
00:04:06,442 --> 00:04:10,983
Because S P M needs computers to build its three dimensional images,

59
00:04:10,983 --> 00:04:15,271
while electron microscopes could record on photographic film.

60
00:04:19,171 --> 00:04:21,117
Here is the summary of topic six.

61
00:04:21,536 --> 00:04:24,583
Grains nucleate, grow and meet at grain boundaries.

62
00:04:25,004 --> 00:04:29,513
Boundaries carry extra energy, so they are reactive and collect impurities.

63
00:04:29,933 --> 00:04:33,187
Stacking faults are a mistake in the stacking order, and

64
00:04:33,187 --> 00:04:36,603
volume defects include inclusions, pores and cracks.

65
00:04:37,023 --> 00:04:40,959
And each microscope has its job: optical, S E M for

66
00:04:40,959 --> 00:04:45,896
surfaces, T E M for thin samples, and S P M for topography.

67
00:04:45,896 --> 00:04:50,033
Next, how atoms move through solids: diffusion.
